# Flexible, Luminescent Metal-Organic Frameworks Showing Synergistic Solid-Solution Effects on Porosity and Sensitivity

## abstract
Mixing molecular building blocks in the solid solution manner is a valuable strategy to obtain structures and properties in between the isostructural parent metal-organic frameworks (MOFs). We report nonlinear/synergistic solid-solution effects using highly related yet non-isostructural, phosphorescent CuI triazolate frameworks as parent phases. Near the phase boundaries associated with conformational diversity and ligand heterogeneity, the porosity (+150 %) and optical O2 sensitivity (410 times, limit of detection 0.07 ppm) can be drastically improved from the best-performing parent MOFs and even exceeds the records hold by precious-metal complexes (3 ppm) and C70 (0.2 ppm).

Porous coordination polymers (PCPs) or metal–organic of the vast diversity and feasible tailorability of their structures and functions compared with conventional porous materials.[1] Since on-demand supramolecular assembly is still very challenging, isoreticular synthesis (varying building blocks within a known MOF prototype or metal–ligand connectivity) is so far the most reliable strategy to obtain desired properties.[2] When isoreticular MOFs possess the same crystal symmetry and very similar lattice parameters, the corresponding geometrically equivalent building blocks can be mixed to construct solid-solution frameworks (SSFs) with structures lying between the parent MOFs.[3] Compared with ordered MOFs, SSFs have additional variables (ingredient, proportion and even space distribution of the randomly distributed building blocks) that can be facilely tailored to fit practical requirements,[4] such as tuning the threshold $\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}$ adsorption/desorption pressures for different heat sources in adsorption cooling applications,[5] and tuning the sensitivity of optical $\mathbf{O}_{2}$ sensor for different target environments.[6]
With suitable mixing ratio of building blocks, SSFs can delicately balance the advantages/shortages of the parent MOFs for better applications.[7] For instance, Kitagawa et al. showed that the high adsorption selectivity of CID-5 and high adsorption capacity of CID-6 can be combined in the corresponding SSFs, giving gas separation efficiencies higher than both of the parent MOFs.[8] Besides linear combinations of the structures and properties of parent MOFs, properly mixed building blocks (types and distributions) may cooperate to give extraordinary performances and even new functions (synergistic effect).[9] Here, we report a unique series of mixed-ligand SSFs showing interesting compositiondependent structures and properties, including superior porosity and $\mathbf{O}_{2}$ sensitivity outperforming the parent MOFs. $\mathbf{O}_{2}$ sensing is important in many fields including, for example, biology, medicine, chemistry, and industry, in which the required detection range varies from ppm level to ambient pressure).[10] As an optical $\mathbf{O}_{2}$ sensing MOF,[11] [Cu(detz)] (MAF-2; Hdetz $^{=3,5}$ -diethyl-1,2,4-triazole) is unique for its precious-metal-free and phosphorescence characters,[12] as well as the highest sensitivity (quenching efficiency of the system, $K_{\mathrm{sv}}$ of $356{\mathrm{bar}}^{-1}$ , defined by the SV equation Eq-S1 in the Supporting Information), but the corresponding preferable detection range (PDR)[13] of 3.5–14 mbar and limit of detection (LOD) of 0.028 mbar or $28\mathrm{ppm}$ , is suitable for neither $\mathbf{O}_{2}$ -free $(<1\mathrm{ppm})$ nor ambient-air (ca. 210 mbar, varies with air pressure and wind speed) environments. According to the luminescence quenching mechanisms (EqS1),[14] $\mathbf{O}_{2}$ sensitivity is proportional with the gas permeability or the host porosity, which inspired us to change the pendant ethyl groups of MAF-2 to larger or smaller ones.
We first replaced the ethyl groups of MAF-2 to propyl groups, by heating mixtures of $\mathrm{Cu}_{2}\mathrm{O}$ nanoparticles and Hdetz/ Hdptz $\mathbf{\nabla}\cdot\operatorname{Hdptz}=3{,}5.$ -dipropyl-1,2,4-triazole) in water to synthesize microcrystalline $\mathrm{\small{[Cu(detz)_{(100-x)\%}(d p t z)_{\boldsymbol{x}\%}]}}$ (denoted as $\mathrm{P}x$ :MAF-2) samples (see Figure S1 and Table S1 in the Supporting Information). Unlike other SSFs varying structures within only one crystal system, $\mathrm{P}x$ :MAF-2 exhibits two crystal systems and a variety of obviously different unit-cell parameters (Figures 1, S1 and Table S2). $\scriptstyle{\mathrm{[Cu(dptz)]}}$ (MAF2P), that is, P100:MAF-2, is a propyl analog of MAF-2. MAF2 and MAF-2P are isoreticular 3-connected nbo-a networks based on 3-coordinated ${\mathrm{Cu}}^{\mathrm{I}}$ and triazolate or 4-connected nbo networks based on planar $\mathbf{C}\mathbf{u}_{2}(\mathrm{triazolate})_{2}$ units. The more porous MAF-2 is flexible, which usually adopts the compressed trigonal form (crystallographic void ratio $=31\%$ ) and can also expand $4\%$ to the most-symmetric cubic form (void ratio $=38\%$ ) after adsorption of benzene.[15] The less porous MAF-2P (void ratio $=14\%$ ) is rigid and only adopts the cubic form, because of the steric hindrance effect of the bulkier propyl groups.[16] At low propyl doping ratios, $\mathbf{P}x$ :MAF-2 exhibits as phase-pure trigonal structures with unit-cell volumes increasing with an increase in $x$ (Figure S1 and
Table S2), because the flexible $\mathrm{Cu}^{\mathrm{I}}$ triazolate scaffold expands to fit the larger space requirement of the propyl groups. When $x\geq46$ , $\mathrm{P}x{\mathrm{:MAF-}}2$ adopts the cubic structure identical with MAF-2P. The boundary between the cubic and trigonal phases is not clear (because the trigonal symmetry is compatible with the cubic one, and the ligand distribution is not completely ordered), which may locate at $28<x<100$ (not just at $28<x<46$ ).
The intrinsic luminescence spectra and the lifetimes of $\mathrm{P}x$ :MAF-2 $(x>0)$ are very similar to those previously reported for MAF-2 (Figures S1–8 and Table S1). Next, their $\mathbf{O}_{2}$ sensitivities were measured in a vacuum- $\mathbf{O}_{2}$ system (Figure 1).[12] Indeed, the least porous P100:MAF-2 possesses the lowest sensitivity $(K_{\mathrm{SV}}=120(1)\mathrm{bar}^{-1}$ , $\mathbf{PDR}=2.1-$ 33 mbar, $\mathrm{LOD}=0.084~\mathrm{mbar},$ ), but is not low enough for ambient-air environments. Unlike the ordered MOFs MAF-2 and MAF-2P, the SSFs $\left(0<x<100\right)$ possess nonlinear SV plots, which are usually attributed to the existence of more than one sensing component (sample heterogeneity or uneven distribution of ligands in this case). More interestingly, near the phase boundary, $\mathbf{P}x{\mathrm{:MAF-}}2$ exhibits an abnormally high $\mathbf{O}_{2}$ sensitivity which cannot be explained by conventional mechanisms (according to Eq-S1 and linear combinations of the structures of MAF-2 and MAF-2P) and indicates the emergence of a synergistic effect. For instance, the $\mathbf{O}_{2}$ sensitivity of P46:MAF-2 $\mathrm{'}K_{\mathrm{SV}}{=}1698\mathrm{bar}^{-1},$ ) is about $370\%$ higher than that of MAF-2.
To further reduce the $\mathbf{O}_{2}$ sensitivity, we replaced the propyl groups of MAF-2P to butyl groups and synthesized $\mathrm{\Delta[Cu(dptz)_{(100-x)\%}(d b t z)_{\boldsymbol{x}\%}]}$ (denoted as $\mathbf{B}\boldsymbol{x}$ :MAF-2P, Hdbtz $\c=$ 3,5-dibutyl-1,2,4-triazole) (Figure S9 and Table S1). [Cu(dbtz)], that is, B100:MAF-2P, possesses the same local coordination geometries and the same planar $\mathbf{C}\mathbf{u}_{2}(\mathrm{triazolate})_{2}$ units as for MAF-2 and MAF-2P, but these building blocks interconnect to form a 3-connected lvt-a or 4-connected lvt topology with tetragonal symmetry and negligible porosity.[17] $\mathtt{B}x$ :MAF-2P possesses a simple “phase diagram” (Figure 2), which contains three regions, that is, the cubic phase at $x\leq47$ , the tetragonal phase at $x\geq73$ , and mechanical mixtures of the tetragonal and cubic phases at $47<x<73$ (Figure S9 and Table S1). Since B100:MAF-2P and MAF-2P possess different metal–ligand connectivities, there is no intermediate lattice conformation between them.
The luminescence and $\mathbf{O}_{2}$ sensing properties of phasepure $\mathbf{B}\boldsymbol{x}$ :MAF-2P samples were studied. The tetragonal $\mathbf{B}\boldsymbol{x}$ :MAF-2P samples $(x\geq73)$ exhibit similar luminescence spectra $\lambda_{\mathrm{ex}}\approx290\mathrm{nm}$ , $\lambda_{\mathrm{em}}\approx470\mathrm{nm}$ and emission lifetimes in vacuum $\tau\approx40\upmu s)$ , while those of the cubic phase $(x\le47,\lambda_{\mathrm{ex}}$ ${\approx}290~\mathrm{nm}$ , $\lambda_{\mathrm{em}}\approx510\mathrm{nm}$ , $\tau\approx100~\ensuremath{\upmu\mathrm{s}},$ are similar with those of MAF-2 (Figure S9 and Table S1). The $\mathbf{O}_{2}$ sensing properties of ${\bf B}x{\bf:M A F-}2{\bf P}$ samples are simple, which show linear SV plots with sensitivities consistent well with Eq-S1 and their void ratios (Figures S10–17). The absence of bended SV plots for the $\mathbf{B}\boldsymbol{x}$ :MAF-2P samples demonstrates that SSFs do not necessarily show bended SV plots. In other words, the bended SV plots of $\mathrm{P}x$ :MAF-2 SSFs are more likely originated from a special effect instead of the sample heterogeneity.
We further synthesized $\mathrm{\Delta[Cu(detz)_{(100-x)\%}(d b t z)_{\boldsymbol{x}\%}]}$ (denoted as $\mathbf{B}\boldsymbol{x}$ :MAF-2) to study the SSF effects on the structure and $\mathbf{O}_{2}$ sensitivity (Figure S18 and Table S1). The “phase diagram” of $\mathbf{B}\boldsymbol{x}$ :MAF-2 can be regarded as a combination of those of $\mathbf{P}x$ :MAF-2 and $\mathbf{B}\boldsymbol{x}$ :MAF-2P, which sequentially changes from the variable trigonal phase, pure cubic phase, tetragonal/cubic mixtures, and the pure tetragonal phase along with the gradual increase of $x$ from 0 to 100 (Figures 3, S18 and Table S2). The observation of the cubic phase in $\mathbf{B}\boldsymbol{x}$ :MAF-2 highlights an exceptional nonlinear SSF effect, as the parent phases are trigonal and tetragonal instead of cubic. As expected, the intrinsic photoluminescence properties of $\mathtt{B}x$ :MAF-2 in the trigonal/cubic and tetragonal phases are similar with those of MAF-2 and B100:MAF-2P, respectively, and their $\mathbf{O}_{2}$ sensing behaviors are also similar with those observed for $\mathrm{P}x$ :MAF-2 and $\mathbf{B}\boldsymbol{x}$ :MAF-2P, including linear SV plots for the tetragonal phase and nonlinear SV plots for the cubic/trigonal phase (Figures S18-23). The sensitivities of $\mathbf{B}\boldsymbol{x}$ :MAF-2 near the cubic and trigonal phases $\stackrel{\cdot}{x}=20$ and 39) are also higher than for MAF-2 (Figure 3). Obviously, among the three studied mixed-ligand systems, the main difference between the tetragonal lvt and the cubic/trigonal nbo phases is that the crystal lattices are constant for the former but component-dependent for the latter. In other words, the exceptionally high $\mathbf{O}_{2}$ sensitivities observed near the cubic/trigonal boundaries are very likely related to the flexibility of the nbo networks.[18] In addition, by virtue of the low and tunable porosities, B100:MAF-2 and B93:MAF-2 exhibit low sensitivities of $K_{\mathrm{SV}}{=}3.6$ bar1 $\mathbf{\mathrm{\mathrm{PDR}}}=69-1111\mathbf{\mathrm{~mba}}$ ) and $K_{\mathrm{sv}}{=}7.0\mathrm{bar}^{-1}$ $(\mathrm{PDR}=35-$ 571 mbar), being useful under ambient-air conditions.
To attain higher $\mathbf{O}_{2}$ sensitivities, we further replaced the ethyl groups of MAF-2 to methyl groups and synthesized $\mathrm{[Cu(dmtz)_{\boldsymbol{x}^{\circ}(\mathrm{detz})_{\boldsymbol{(100-x)}^{\circ}\%}}]}$ (Mx:MAF-2, Hdmtz $\scriptstyle=3,5.$ - dimethyl-1,2,4-trizaole) (Figures $\mathtt{4a}$ , S24 and Table S1). Note that three isomers of $\mathrm{[Cu(dmtz)]}$ have been previously documented,[19] but they are all nonporous and structurally very different with MAF-2. Unexpectedly, introducing the smaller methyl groups leads to gradual expansion of the unitcell volume $(x<56)$ and even completely to the cubic structure $(56\leq x\leq76$ , Figure S24), just similar with the bulkier propyl/butyl groups for $\mathbf{P}x{\mathrm{:MAF-}}2$ and $\mathbf{B}\boldsymbol{x}$ :MAF-2. Obviously, the small methyl groups cannot play the steric hindrance role. Instead, this phenomenon indicates attractive interactions between adjacent ethyl groups in MAF-2, which stabilize the trigonal conformation. When $x>76$ , ${\bf M}x$ :MAF-2 exhibits as an unknown structure with poor crystallinity (Figure S24), being consistent with the fact that $\mathrm{[Cu(dmtz)]}$ cannot crystallize as the nbo structure (Figure 4 a).[18]
The intrinsic photoluminescence and composition-dependent $\mathbf{O}_{2}$ sensing properties of $\mathbf{M}x$ :MAF-2 $(0<x\leq76)$ are similar to those of the nbo analogs described above (Figures S24–32). However, due to the higher porosities and flexibilities, the $\mathbf{O}_{2}$ sensitivities of Mx:MAF-2 samples are extraordinarily high. As shown in Figure $\mathtt{4a}$ , the void ratio and $\mathbf{O}_{2}$ sensitivity of ${\bf M}x$ :MAF-2 increase in similar trends when $x$ changes from 0 to 30, being consistent with the conventional mechanism (Eq-S1). Near the phase boundary, the $\mathbf{O}_{2}$ sensitivity increases much faster than the void ratio does, and reaches an exceptionally high $K_{\mathrm{SV}}$ of $9.8(6)\times$ $10^{4}\mathrm{bar}^{-1}$ $\mathrm{\langleLOD=0.103(6)ppm)}$ at $x=66$ . The $\mathrm{O}_{2}$ sensitivity of M76:MAF-2 $(K_{\mathrm{SV}}=9.2(6)\times10^{4}\mathrm{bar}^{-1})$ is slightly lower than for M66:MAF-2, which may be ascribed to the deviation from phase boundary and/or presence of small amount of the unknown structure for $x>76$ .
An $\mathrm{\bfO}_{2}/\mathrm{\bfN}_{2}$ mixed-gas system was further used (for detailed explanations, see the Supporting Information) to accurately measure the extremely high $\mathbf{O}_{2}$ sensitivities as $K_{\mathrm{SV}}=1.48(3)\times$ $10^{5}\mathrm{bar}^{-1}$ ( $\mathrm{{LOD=0.07\ppm})}$ and $1.20(2)\times10^{5}\mathrm{bar}^{-1}$ $\mathrm{\DeltaLOD=}$ $0.08\mathrm{ppm})$ for M66:MAF-2 and M76:MAF-2, respectively (Figures S33 and S34), being 410 times higher than for MAF2. Note that the sensitivities of these precious-metal-free phosphorescence materials well exceed the known records hold by precious-metal complexes $\left(\mathrm{LOD}=3\mathrm{ppm}\right)$ ) and the very expensive $\mathrm{\mathbf{C}}_{70}$ $\mathrm{\langleLOD=}0.2\mathrm{ppm}$ , see Table 1).[20] The $\mathbf{O}_{2}$ sensing of M66:MAF-2 also exhibits good linearity and very fast response and recovery times $(<5~\mathrm{s})$ below 5 ppm $\mathbf{O}_{2}$ (Figures $\boldsymbol{4\mathrm{b}}$ and S35).
M66:MAF-2 exhibits linear and reversible $\mathbf{O}_{2}$ adsorption/ desorption isotherms at $298\mathrm{K}$ with an uptake of $0.13\mathrm{mmol}\mathrm{g}^{-1}$ at 1 bar (Figure S36), corresponding to a solubility of $0.15\mathrm{molL}^{-1}$ or stoichiometry of $0.80\mathrm{O}_{2}/\mathrm{Cu}$ . These values are larger than those of MAF-2 $\mathbf{\hat{\mu}}_{0.12}\mathbf{mol}\mathbf{L}^{-1}$ , $0.56\mathrm{O}_{2}/$ Cu) with stronger confinement effect (smaller pore),[12] indicating a stronger $\mathrm{O}_{2}$ binding affinity of M66:MAF-2. However, the increases of the $\mathrm{O}_{2}$ solubility and binding affinity is not sufficient to explain the extraordinarily high $\mathbf{O}_{2}$ sensitivity of M66:MAF-2, and further implies the important role of the framework flexibility.
$\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ adsorption isotherm of M66:MAF-2 at $195\mathrm{K}$ exhibits three unobvious steps with apparent pore volumes of 0.30, 0.43, and $0.59\mathrm{cm}^{3}\mathrm{g}^{-1}$ (Figures 4c and S37). Considering that the crystallographic value is $0.48\mathrm{cm}^{3}\mathbf{g}^{-1}$ for M66:MAF-2, the first and third steps indicate framework contraction and expansion at low and high gas pressures, respectively. The porosity of M66:MAF-2 is much higher than all ordered $\mathrm{Cu^{\mathrm{I}}}$ triazolate frameworks,[5,16,19,21] and about $150\%$ higher than the most porous one, MAF-2 (crystallographic/measured pore volumes are $0.26/0.23\mathrm{cm}^{3}\mathrm{g}^{-1}\mathrm{\Delta}$ .[21] Compared with M66:MAF2, M76:MAF-2 exhibits lower $\mathbf{CO}_{2}$ uptakes, indicating weaker framework flexibility and/or the presence of small amount of unknown structure at $x>76$ . Besides, M100:MAF-2 can only adsorb $20\mathrm{cm}^{3}\mathrm{g}^{-1}$ of $\mathbf{CO}_{2}$ at $195\mathrm{K}$ (Figure S37), being indicative of a host structure with a very low porosity and a topology completely different with the nbo counterparts.
In summary, in this work we studied a series of mixedligand SSFs based on four highly related phosphorescent ${\mathrm{Cu}}^{\mathrm{I}}$ dialkyl-1,2,4-triazolate frameworks possessing multiple metal–ligand connectivities and network conformations (crystal symmetries). The SSFs with two non-isoreticular parent phases (e.g. Bx:MAF-2P) show unaltered network conformation and simple linear combination effect within each phase, which can finely reduce the $\mathbf{O}_{2}$ sensitivity to fit ambient-air environments. More interestingly, the SSFs with isoreticular but not isostructural parent phases (e.g. $\mathrm{P}x$ :MAF-2 and Mx:MAF-2) show variable network conformations and exceptionally high porosities and $\mathbf{O}_{2}$ sensitivities near the phase boundaries, indicating cooperative/synergistic relationships between the phase transition of the flexible MOF and the ligand heterogeneity. These results can provide new insights and strategies for controlling and elevating the properties of MOFs.
